	<xs:complexType name="ComparableRankingType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Used to compare different items (Task, Capability Command, Capability Activity, etc.). This is accomplished by first comparing two ComparableRankingTypes' priority values. In the event of the items having equal priority values the precedence values are then compared. For example, Task A has a (priority, precedence) value pair of (4, 10), Task B has a (priority, precedence) value pair of (4, 23), Task C has a (priority, precedence) value pair of (7, 2): the ranking order would be first Task A, then Task B, then Task C. Since Task A and Task B have the same relative priority value, Task A will be ranked before Task B because Task A has a higher relative precedence value. Task C will be ranked after Tasks A and B because it has a lower relative priority value than Tasks A and B.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Priority" type="xs:unsignedShort">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Priority value of an item (Task, Capability Command, Capability Activity, etc.).  Any item with a numerically lower priority value must be considered prior to any items with a numerically higher priority value.  Priority doesn't necessarily dictate temporal ordering.  A value of zero is highest priority, next highest is one, etc. Priority changes monotonically. For example, given values A=6, B=4, C=3, the order should be first C, then B, then A, with C being the highest relative priority.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="PrecedenceWithinPriority" type="xs:unsignedShort">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Precedence value of an item (Task, Capability Command, Capability Activity, etc.).  Any item with a numerically lower precedence value must be considered prior to any items with a numerically higher precedence value.  Precedence doesn't necessarily dictate temporal ordering.  A value of zero is highest precedence, next highest is one, etc. Precedence changes monotonically. For example, given values A=6, B=4, C=3, the order should be first C, then B, then A, with C being the highest relative precedence.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
